{
  "levels": [
    {
      "id": "sample",
      "title": "Sample unit",
      "story": "This is a test page for the game's builders. It uses every activity type once, with a pizza shop as the story, so we can check each one works on a laptop and on a phone.",
      "everyday": {
        "scene": "You order a pizza on your phone. A few minutes later a driver arrives with it, still hot, and they found your street without asking you for directions.",
        "question": "How did the shop turn your order into a hot pizza at your door?",
        "answer": "By breaking the job into steps in the right order, sorting out the toppings, working out the price, and finding a route that reaches every house. This unit practises each of those ideas."
      },
      "lessons": [
        {
          "id": "steps-order",
          "title": "Steps come in an order",
          "text": [
            "A recipe is a list of steps, and the order matters. You can't slice a pizza before you bake it, and you can't bake it before you add the sauce. In computing, a list of steps that solves a problem is called an algorithm.",
            "Kit's first job at the shop: put the steps in the right order."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "Algorithm",
              "meaning": "A step-by-step method for solving a problem."
            }
          ],
          "predict": {
            "prompt": "Can you add the cheese after the pizza is baked and still get a normal pizza?",
            "answer": "Not really. The cheese needs to melt in the oven, so it has to go on before baking."
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: Recipe",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recipe"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "names-and-groups",
          "title": "Names and groups",
          "text": [
            "Every job has its own words. A topping is food that goes on top of the pizza, the crust is the bread edge, and a slice is one piece. Knowing the words helps you read an order.",
            "Toppings also fall into groups. Mushrooms, peppers and olives are vegetables. Ham, salami and chicken are meat. Mozzarella and cheddar are cheese."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "Topping",
              "meaning": "Food that goes on top of a pizza."
            },
            {
              "term": "Crust",
              "meaning": "The bread base and edge of a pizza."
            },
            {
              "term": "Slice",
              "meaning": "One piece of a cut pizza."
            }
          ],
          "table": {
            "head": [
              "Group",
              "Examples"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Vegetable",
                "mushroom, pepper, olive"
              ],
              [
                "Meat",
                "ham, salami, chicken"
              ],
              [
                "Cheese",
                "mozzarella, cheddar"
              ]
            ]
          },
          "diagramCaption": "Three groups of toppings.",
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: Pizza",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pizza"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "sharing-maths",
          "title": "Sharing and prices",
          "text": [
            "Pizza maths is everyday maths. A pizza cut into 8 slices has 8 slices, so if 3 friends eat 2 slices each, they eat 3 × 2 = 6, and 8 − 6 = 2 are left.",
            "Prices work the same way. Three pizzas at 12 each cost 3 × 12 = 36. A discount of 10% takes off one tenth: 36 × 0.9 = 32.40. You can use the calculator for sums like these."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "Percent (%)",
              "meaning": "Out of 100. 10% means 10 out of every 100, or one tenth."
            }
          ],
          "deeper": {
            "title": "Counting choices",
            "text": [
              "Each topping can be on or off. With 2 toppings there are 2 × 2 = 4 combinations. With 4 toppings there are 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 2⁴ = 16. Every extra topping doubles the number of pizzas you could order."
            ]
          },
          "predict": {
            "prompt": "A pizza has 8 slices and you eat 3. How many are left?",
            "answer": "5, because 8 − 3 = 5."
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: Percentage",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Percentage"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "delivery-map",
          "title": "Finding every house",
          "text": [
            "The driver's map is a set of houses joined by roads. Computer scientists call a map like this a graph: the houses are nodes and the roads are edges.",
            "One good way to visit every house is nearest first. Visit the shop, add every house you can reach from it to a waiting list, then visit them in the order they joined the list. This is called breadth-first search."
          ],
          "terms": [
            {
              "term": "Graph",
              "meaning": "Points (nodes) joined by lines (edges)."
            },
            {
              "term": "Node",
              "meaning": "One point in a graph, like a house."
            },
            {
              "term": "Edge",
              "meaning": "A line joining two nodes, like a road."
            }
          ],
          "predict": {
            "prompt": "The driver is at the shop. Which houses go on the waiting list first?",
            "answer": "Every house with a road straight from the shop."
          },
          "references": [
            {
              "title": "Wikipedia: Breadth-first search",
              "source": "Wikipedia (linked, not copied)",
              "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Breadth-first_search"
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
